Dry Mouth (Xerostomia)
Palliative & End of Life
Distressing oral dryness, Thick stringy saliva, Difficulty chewing/swallowing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-022
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting oral pain and absolute inability to swallow oral medications due to profound mucosal dryness and cracking.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, registered nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient was experiencing agonizing, continuous burning in the mouth with severe cracking and bleeding of the lips, completely preventing the administration of their essential regular opioid and anticonvulsant medications. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to diagnose and treat the suspected florid oral candidiasis and severe xerostomia, and to provide alternative parenteral routes for missed medications to prevent a systemic pain crisis or withdrawal prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My mouth feels like it is full of broken glass. I can't chew, I can't swallow my pills, and my tongue is burning constantly."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a profound, continuous, and highly distressing subjective sensation of a severely dry oral cavity over the past week. There is distinct, severe difficulty chewing, explicitly initiating a functional swallow, and articulating completely clear speech. The nurse notes thick, extremely stringy, or noticeably ropey saliva physically pooling within the oral cavity, alongside noticeable, severe halitosis directly secondary to diminished salivary flow and increased oral bacterial load. The patient complains of an uncomfortable, continuous burning sensation explicitly localizing to the tongue and buccal mucosa. The patient has a significantly altered taste sensation (dysgeusia), manifesting as a persistent metallic flavor profile. Today, the patient has developed cracking, peeling, and severe generalized erythema strictly affecting the lips and precise corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis).
Functional Status: Fully conscious but completely refusing all oral intake, fluids, and medications.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration, malnutrition, and acute medication withdrawal due to the absolute inability to swallow pills.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient is visibly uncomfortable, frequently attempting to clear their throat, with audible smacking sounds from the sticky oral mucosa.
Physical Exam: Thorough clinical physical examination explicitly identifies absent salivary pooling in the floor of the mouth definitively confirming severe localized salivary gland hypofunction. The tongue is fissured, distinctly lobulated, and severely erythematous, presenting completely devoid of normal protective moisture. There are white, adherent plaques on the buccal mucosa indicative of florid oral candidiasis. The corners of the mouth show deep, erythematous fissures. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the highly distressing oral dryness (absent pooling), the visible thick stringy saliva, and the observed severe difficulty chewing/swallowing.
Environmental Observations: A full cup of untouched water and unadministered oral medications sit on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient is on multiple regular anticholinergic medications (amitriptyline, hyoscine) which are the primary iatrogenic drivers of this severe xerostomia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dry Mouth (Xerostomia) - Status: Exacerbated (Complicated by Oral Candidiasis)
Differentials:

Viral stomatitis (Herpes simplex) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of distinct, fluid-filled vesicles or classic ulcerations; the presentation is classic for fungal overgrowth and severe dryness.

Mucositis (chemotherapy-induced) - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient has not received cytotoxic chemotherapy or head/neck radiation in the past 12 months.

Vitamin B12/Folate deficiency - Ruled out/unlikely to be the sole cause of the sudden, acute ropey saliva and thick candidal plaques, though glossitis can look similar.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from an acute, agonizing exacerbation of medication-induced xerostomia, now severely complicated by florid oral candidiasis and angular cheilitis. The objective findings of a completely desiccated, erythematous oral cavity with stringy saliva and fungal plaques perfectly explain the severe pain and absolute inability to swallow. Because the patient is entirely refusing their essential baseline palliative medications due to this oral pain, delaying assessment until standard hours would directly precipitate a severe systemic pain crisis and withdrawal. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was absolutely required to bypass the oral route with parenteral analgesia tonight, and to initiate immediate topical antifungal and artificial saliva therapies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous morphine to cover the missed oral opioid doses and relieve the acute oral pain. Prescribed nystatin oral drops (to be swished and swallowed, bypassing the need to chew) and miconazole gel for the angular cheilitis. Initiated regular artificial saliva sprays and a comprehensive oral care protocol.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel your mouth becoming sticky (trigger), explicitly use the prescribed artificial saliva spray immediately, rather than trying to force a dry swallow (behavior). This will lubricate the tissues, preventing them from sticking together and tearing, which directly reduces the burning pain (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued facility nursing care with strict instructions for 2-hourly mouth care.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of a soft-bristled toothbrush and non-foaming, SLS-free toothpaste.
Follow-up: Facility GP review in 48 hours to assess the resolution of the candidiasis and review the baseline anticholinergic burden.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a high fever, sudden swelling of the neck or jaw, or remains completely unable to swallow even their own saliva or the liquid antifungal medication after 24 hours, the nursing staff must contact the after-hours medical service.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The dryness is largely a side effect of necessary pain and nerve medications; it cannot be entirely cured, but it can be aggressively managed.
Clinical Course: The fungal infection should clear within 5-7 days of continuous topical treatment, restoring the ability to swallow soft foods and pills safely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff: Do not attempt to force solid pills. Crush medications and mix with a small amount of fruit puree if tolerated, or request subcutaneous alternatives from the GP tomorrow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your pain and nerve medications have completely dried up your saliva, allowing a yeast infection to overgrow and burn your mouth. We gave you an injection for the pain tonight so you don't have to swallow a pill. The drops and gels will kill the yeast and act like artificial spit to keep your mouth wet and comfortable.
Actionable Steps: Swish the fungal drops around your mouth for as long as possible before swallowing. Avoid any acidic, spicy, or dry, crumbly foods.

SMS INFORMATION

